The C# code to output an assert statement based on the comment can be written as follows:

```cs
public void PUT_CountContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

    int New_Ret = arrList.Count;

    PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);

    // Using Debug.Assert to ensure the count value is either 0 or greater than or equal to 1
    Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0);
}
```

In this code, we use the `Debug.Assert` to check if the count of the `arrList` is either 0 or greater than or equal to 1. The `Debug.Assert` statement is only executed if the condition inside it is true. If the condition is false, then it triggers a break point in the execution.
--------------------The problem here is to create an Assert statement that checks if the "New_Ret" value is 0 or greater. Here is how you might create these Assert statements:

```cs
namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_CountContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int New_Ret = arrList.Count;

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);

            // @@@ count value of array is either 0 or greater than or equal to one 
            Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && New_Ret <= 1, "The count value of the array is not in the proper range (0 or greater)");
        }
    }
}
```

This code creates an Assert statement that checks if the value of "New_Ret" is greater than or equal to 0 and less than or equal to 1. If the condition is not met, a runtime error will occur with the message "The count value of the array is not in the proper range (0 or greater)".
